2. ARCHITECTURE AND CORE DESIGN PRINCIPLES
====================================================================

Use a provider-adapter design. The Notification Hub must not hard-code a single email vendor or WhatsApp provider into business logic.

Expected application stack, subject to the existing project's actual stack:
- Frontend: React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind CSS, or the established frontend stack already in the project.
- Backend: FastAPI + Python + Pydantic + SQLAlchemy 2.x, or the established backend stack already in the project.
- Database: PostgreSQL with versioned schema migrations, or the database already approved for the project.
- Background delivery: durable database-backed queue with worker processes; use the project's approved queue technology if one already exists.
- Secrets: environment variables or an approved secrets manager. Store only secret references or encrypted values using a well-defined secrets-management design; never expose raw credentials to the browser.
- Time: store timestamps in UTC; render them in the user's configured timezone.
- Audit: record actor, action, timestamp, target, outcome and relevant configuration/version identifiers. Do not log passwords, access tokens, API secrets, full authorization headers or unnecessary personal message content.

Required logical components:
A. Notification Orchestrator
B. Application Event Registry
C. Rule and Recipient Resolver
D. Channel Eligibility / Consent Evaluator
E. Template Renderer and Validator
F. Durable Notification Queue
G. Email Provider Adapter
H. WhatsApp Provider Adapter
I. In-app Notification Centre
J. Delivery Receipt / Webhook Processor
K. Retry and Dead-Letter Manager
L. Broadcast Campaign Scheduler
M. Audit and Observability Layer
N. Reporting and Reconciliation Services

The application event should create a notification intent first. It should not synchronously send email or WhatsApp inside an ordinary business transaction. Persist the business transaction and notification intent safely, preferably using a transactional outbox or equivalent pattern, then process it asynchronously. Define idempotency keys so duplicate application events, repeated API requests, worker restarts or duplicate webhooks do not create duplicate sends.

Use database transactions, row locking or equivalent concurrency-safe claiming, retry-safe state transitions, bounded retries and monitoring. Multiple workers must not process the same queued message simultaneously.

