Queue states should be explicitly documented. A recommended internal lifecycle is:
CREATED → VALIDATED → QUEUED → PROCESSING → SUBMITTED_TO_PROVIDER → ACCEPTED_BY_PROVIDER → DELIVERED (if confirmed) → READ (if supported and confirmed).
Possible alternate terminal or exceptional states:
FAILED_RETRYABLE, FAILED_PERMANENT, RETRY_SCHEDULED, CANCELLED, SUPPRESSED, EXPIRED, DEAD_LETTER, UNKNOWN, USER_ACTION_REQUIRED, OPERATOR_REPORTED_SENT.

Do not force every provider/channel to support every state. Map provider-specific statuses to internal statuses through a documented mapping. Preserve the original provider status and event time. “Submitted to provider”, “accepted”, “delivered” and “read” are distinct. If no delivery receipt exists, status must remain at the last verified state or UNKNOWN rather than being fabricated.

Queue functions:
- Search by internal ID, provider ID, recipient reference or correlation ID subject to authorization.
- Filter by channel, status, date, event, template, campaign, priority, retry count and provider.
- Sort and paginate server-side.
- View message lifecycle timeline.
- View sanitized error details and retry history.
- Retry an eligible failed message after permission and policy checks.
- Cancel a queued/scheduled message only if it has not crossed a non-cancellable state.
- Replay eligible messages with idempotency protection.
- Export authorized operational data with audit logging and data minimization.
- Bulk retry only for authorized users, with a preview, count and confirmation.
- Bulk cancel only where safe and allowed, with preview and confirmation.
- Show queue age and oldest item.
- Display stale/locked processing jobs and safe recovery actions.
- Do not allow operators to change recipient/content invisibly on a queued record. Corrections must create a new version or controlled replacement with audit and approval.

