15. RETRIES, BACKOFF, DEAD-LETTER QUEUE AND RECOVERY
====================================================================

Implement a durable retry manager:
- Classify failures as transient, permanent, policy/eligibility, configuration, rate-limit, authentication, provider outage, invalid recipient, template rejection and unknown.
- Use bounded exponential backoff with jitter where appropriate.
- Respect provider Retry-After and rate-limit instructions.
- Define maximum attempts, maximum message age and expiry behavior by channel/event category.
- Do not retry permanent failures without corrective action.
- Do not retry messages that are now ineligible due to opt-out, revoked consent, template invalidation or policy restrictions.
- Avoid retrying a request if provider acceptance is ambiguous unless the adapter supports a safe idempotency mechanism or reconciliation procedure.
- Do not create duplicates after a worker timeout, process crash or webhook replay.
- Use a dead-letter state/queue after retry exhaustion or non-retryable conditions.
- Provide failure reason, attempts, first/last failure time, next action and recovery guidance.
- Support authorized manual retry after validation and explicit confirmation.
- Record who retried, when, why and which configuration/template version was used.
- Alert administrators for repeated provider failures, queue backlog, old pending messages and retry exhaustion.
- Provide a controlled reprocessing operation that preserves the original history and creates a traceable attempt.

Define per-channel/per-event retry policy as configuration, with validation and safe defaults. Do not permit unbounded retries or unbounded queue growth.

